    "created_at": "2021-08-31T19:33:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-08-31T19:33:44Z",
    "body": "This has some subtle overlap with lazyload semantics too. I believe as per https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/images.html#updating-the-image-data, `loading=lazy` images are allowed to load images immediately from the list of available images if they appear in there. This behavior seems reasonable. But as of now per spec, the only images that appear in \"the list\" are ones that have fully finished download. If we change that and allow in-flight image requests (pending images) to appear in the list of available images, this means that both `<imgs>` in the following site will load, even without the user scrolling. This seems fine as well I think, it is just a [bit confusing sometimes](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=994329).\r\n\r\n```\r\n<img src=super-slow.jpg>\r\n<div class=\"way-taller-than-viewport\"></div>\r\n<img src=super-slow.jpg loading=lazy>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn other words, we just maximize the number of lazy loaded resources that will load without ever being scrolled to, given the fact that the underlying resource they request is already being fetched anyways. As I mentioned, I think this is fine but might just be a bit confusing.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
